From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: domicile CI <ci@domicile.invalid>
Date: Thu, 25 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] domicile: a desk nobody is at locks itself

Registers the lock's two members of the control protocol: a `locked` event
carrying whether this desk will deliver a keystroke to a client at all, and
DomicileHost.unlock(), which offers a passphrase at one that will not.

Three lists Chromium owns, and nothing else. The event, its init dictionary,
the method and the mojom members are new files under src/; what cannot be a
new file is a name in a generated-bindings list and a name in the event
registry.

A SEPARATE PATCH RATHER THAN A LINE IN 0009, WHICH IS WHERE THIS SERIES HAS
ALREADY BEEN BITTEN. #528 added `idle` inside 0009's own hunks, which moved
every line below them and left 0039 and 0042 stating offsets into a file that
no longer looked like the one they were generated against. `git am` died on
crux hours into the job. Every feature since 0009 appends a patch of its own
instead, and each one's context is taken from the tree the patch before it
leaves -- which for these three lists means the domicile block as the series
ends, not as the pin has it.

WHAT THE STATE IS FOR. The compositor holds the lock, because input on this
system does not originate there: the shell's page owns it and forwards it, and
the compositor injects it into a Wayland seat. So the lock is the injection not
happening, and the page keeps every key it has -- which is what lets it draw a
lock screen and take a passphrase while no client on the desk sees a keystroke.
A page reload does not open the desk, and neither does a page edited in the
devtools of the browser drawing it.

Adding a member costs a near-full Blink rebuild, because a new inbound message
needs a new event type and that invalidates the generated bindings.
